module b3::draw;

<*
 A colour packed as 0xRRGGBB. Not an enum: box3d's b3HexColor names 145 constants, but the values
 a renderer is handed are not confined to them, box3d passing a shape's custom colour through
 unexamined and packing a debug material preset into the unused high byte.
*>
typedef HexColor = inline uint;

<*
 The number of constraint graph colours, mirroring B3_GRAPH_COLOR_COUNT in constants.h, which the
 C API does not expose as a function. The last of them is the overflow slot.
*>
const int GRAPH_COLOR_COUNT = 24;

<*
 Raw graph colour. Private because graph_color() refuses the out-of-range index box3d does no more
 than assert against before reading past its table.
*>
extern fn HexColor graph_color_raw(int index) @cname("b3GetGraphColor") @private;

<*
 One shape offered to a renderer so it can build whatever it draws that shape with. Read the arm
 the type names inside the call: the sphere, capsule and mesh arms point into the world's shape
 array, which moves when it grows, so a pointer kept is stale from the next shape created. The
 compound and height field arms are the caller's own geometry and outlive the call. The other five
 arms of any one shape are its address read as geometry it is not.
*>
struct DebugShape {
    ShapeId shape_id;
    ShapeType type;
    union {
        Capsule* capsule;
        CompoundData* compound;
        HeightFieldData* height_field;
        HullData* hull;
        Mesh* mesh;
        Sphere* sphere;
    }
}

<*
 Draws one shape at the pose its body holds. user_shape is whatever WorldDef.create_debug_shape
 answered for that shape, which box3d keeps, so it asks once and draws through the answer after.
*>
alias DrawShapeFn = fn void(void* user_shape, WorldTransform transform, HexColor color, void* context);
alias DrawSegmentFn = fn void(Pos p1, Pos p2, HexColor color, void* context);
alias DrawTransformFn = fn void(WorldTransform transform, void* context);
alias DrawPointFn = fn void(Pos p, float size, HexColor color, void* context);
alias DrawSphereFn = fn void(Pos p, float radius, HexColor color, float alpha, void* context);
alias DrawCapsuleFn = fn void(Pos p1, Pos p2, float radius, HexColor color, float alpha, void* context);
alias DrawBoundsFn = fn void(Aabb aabb, HexColor color, void* context);
alias DrawBoxFn = fn void(Vec3 extents, WorldTransform transform, HexColor color, void* context);
alias DrawStringFn = fn void(Pos p, ZString s, HexColor color, void* context);

<*
 What a renderer hands world_draw(): nine callbacks taking world coordinates, the box outside
 which nothing is drawn, and a flag per thing to draw. See default_debug_draw() for the state one
 starts in, and DrawShapeFn for what a shape needs beyond draw_shapes.
*>
struct DebugDraw {
    DrawShapeFn draw_shape_fcn;
    DrawSegmentFn draw_segment_fcn;
    DrawTransformFn draw_transform_fcn;
    DrawPointFn draw_point_fcn;
    DrawSphereFn draw_sphere_fcn;
    DrawCapsuleFn draw_capsule_fcn;
    DrawBoundsFn draw_bounds_fcn;
    DrawBoxFn draw_box_fcn;
    DrawStringFn draw_string_fcn;
    Aabb drawing_bounds;
    float force_scale;
    float joint_scale;
    bool draw_shapes;
    bool draw_joints;
    bool draw_joint_extras;
    bool draw_bounds;
    bool draw_mass;
    bool draw_sleep;
    bool draw_body_names;
    bool draw_contacts;
    bool draw_anchor_a;
    bool draw_graph_colors;
    bool draw_contact_features;
    bool draw_contact_normals;
    bool draw_contact_forces;
    bool draw_islands;
    void* context;
}

<*
 A draw definition with every flag false, so a caller who takes it and draws sees nothing until
 one is raised. The nine callbacks are box3d's own empty bodies rather than null, the drawing
 bounds reach 100 length units either side of the origin, and both scales are 1.
*>
extern fn DebugDraw default_debug_draw() @cname("b3DefaultDebugDraw");

<*
 Draws the world through the definition's callbacks, culled to its drawing bounds and to
 mask_bits, which gates everything: a mask of zero draws nothing at all. Shapes take a second
 switch as well, WorldDef.create_debug_shape, which only world creation can set. box3d tests no
 callback for null before calling it, so a raised flag over a null one is a crash rather than a
 skip, and its check that the drawing bounds are a valid box is an assertion this build
 compiles out.

 @param mask_bits : `the bits a shape's own filter categories must overlap for it to be considered`
*>
extern fn void world_draw(WorldId world, DebugDraw* draw, ulong mask_bits) @cname("b3World_Draw");
